WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.912
Claude Code now supports artifacts.

2
00:00:03.362 --> 00:00:12.996
Preview your in-progress work in Claude Code as a live, interactive artifact, built from your full session context and shareable with your team.

3
00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:17.586
From Claude Blog, published June 18, 2026.

4
00:00:18.586 --> 00:00:36.602
Starting today, Claude Code can capture work progress as an artifact, which turn Claude Code's work into live, shareable visual pages, including P R walkthroughs, system explainers, dashboards, and release checklists, that update themselves as your session works.

5
00:00:37.052 --> 00:00:59.433
A Claude Code session can range from investigating an incident to refactoring a service to analyzing months of data. Artifacts translate the work into a web page anyone can open and explore, like a pull request walkthrough, a dashboard you can filter and sort, or even a release checklist that fills itself out as work gets done.

6
00:00:59.553 --> 00:01:08.978
Artifacts make it easier to collaborate on shared work, so teams can spend more time building and less time communicating status updates.

7
00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:11.933
Built on the context from your session.

8
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:34.956
Claude Code builds an artifact using the full context of your session, including your codebase, your connectors, and the conversation itself. A single incident page can bring together the failing test and the function behind it from your code, the error spike from a connected monitoring tool, and the root-cause reasoning from the session you just ran.

9
00:01:35.076 --> 00:01:45.403
With artifacts, you don't need to wire up data sources or stand up infrastructure. You ask for a page, and Claude Code builds it from what already exists.

10
00:01:46.203 --> 00:01:48.394
Live pages that update in place.

11
00:01:48.844 --> 00:02:08.150
When Claude Code updates an artifact, the open page refreshes in place and teammates see the updates the moment they’re published. Every publish is a new version at the same link, with version history so you can restore at any time, and a gallery lets you browse and manage all artifacts you've made.

12
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:30.991
From our internal testing, one of our most common use cases has been debugging. These typically look something like: An engineer kicks off an incident investigation before standup. Claude Code works through the logs and publishes an artifact: a timeline, the suspect commits, and an error-rate chart. She shares the link with her team from the page header.

13
00:02:31.111 --> 00:02:50.453
By the time standup begins, Claude has republished it twice as the investigation progressed, incorporating the latest information. With artifacts, team members and stakeholders don’t have to "walk us through what the agent found" because they're all looking at the same view, with the same context.

14
00:02:51.253 --> 00:02:52.970
Private to your organization.

15
00:02:53.420 --> 00:03:09.189
Every artifact is private to its author by default. When you're ready, share it with your teammates and your organization directly from the page. Artifacts are viewable only by authenticated members of your org and cannot be made public.

16
00:03:09.309 --> 00:03:20.235
Admins manage access with an org-level toggle and role-based scoping, set retention policies, and get org-wide visibility through the compliance A P I.

17
00:03:21.035 --> 00:03:22.155
Getting started.

18
00:03:22.605 --> 00:03:29.451
Ask your session for an artifact, or just ask for something visual, h ere are some ideas by role:

19
00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:45.070
Legal / open source: A license audit of every dependency, flagging copyleft, straight from the repo. "Build an artifact listing every third-party dependency and its license, flagging anything copyleft.".

20
00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:58.474
Privacy: A data-flow map of where personal data is collected, stored, and logged across the code. "Trace where we touch personal data across the codebase into an artifact for the privacy review.".

21
00:03:58.714 --> 00:04:09.801
Security: Findings that link to the exact line, so the fix is unambiguous. "Build an artifact of the auth findings from this review, each linked to the code.".

22
00:04:10.041 --> 00:04:25.252
FinOps / platform finance: Cloud resources and cost drivers mapped from your infrastructure-as-code. "Map our cloud resources from the Terraform into an artifact, grouped by service, with the big cost drivers.".

23
00:04:25.492 --> 00:04:39.443
Software engineers: A P R or bug walkthrough reviewers can actually follow, pulled from the diff and the code around it. "Make an artifact walking through this P R, the diff, the reasoning, and what I tested.".

24
00:04:39.683 --> 00:04:55.692
Designers and frontend engineers: Several UX directions for a screen, each built from your real components so the one you pick is shippable. "Give me an artifact with 5 UX variations of this signup form, built from our component library.".

25
00:04:55.932 --> 00:05:09.955
Staff engineers and architects: A map of how a service actually fits together, drawn from the real import graph instead of a whiteboard. "Map how the payments service fits together into an artifact, from the code.".

26
00:05:10.195 --> 00:05:26.145
SRE and on-call: An incident page that grows as you investigate and becomes the postmortem. "Turn this incident into an artifact, timeline, suspect commits, error spike from our monitoring, and republish as I work through it.".

27
00:05:26.385 --> 00:05:38.895
Engineering managers: A page of what actually shipped, built from the merged P Rs. "Build an artifact of what merged on my team this week from the P Rs, grouped by project.".

28
00:05:39.345 --> 00:05:50.598
Claude Code builds the page and gives you a link. Open it in your browser or the desktop app, share it from the header, updates publish to the same U R L automatically.

29
00:05:51.398 --> 00:05:52.468
Availability.

30
00:05:52.918 --> 00:06:03.526
Artifacts is available in beta to Claude Team and Enterprise orgs, from the Claude Code C L I and desktop app, with pages viewable in any browser.

31
00:06:03.976 --> 00:06:06.076
Get started today with Claude Code.

32
00:06:07.076 --> 00:06:24.878
That was Claude Code now supports artifacts, from Claude Blog, published June 18, 2026. Read the original at claude dot com. This unofficial audio edition was generated automatically for the podcast Anthropic, Read Aloud.
